% ============================================================================
% flame-colors.query.adj — a worked recall over the chemistry flame-colors library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "what color does sodium give
% in a flame test?": it states no chemistry fact — it only `import`s the grounded
% table and asks binding queries. The engine resolves each `?` against the
% `flame_color` rows and returns the color + the table's source/locator/trust.
% The fourth query runs the relation BACKWARD from a color (bind `red`, recall
% BOTH metals the source gives that color — strontium and lithium, since a flame
% color is not unique to one element). The fifth runs it backward from another
% color (bind `violet`, recall the metal — potassium). A `copper` flame color is
% NOT stated on this source page, so a recall on it abstains honestly — the
% engine never invents a color.
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative import resolves):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli flame-colors.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "flame-colors.adj"

? flame_color(sodium, $Color)      % expect orange
? flame_color(potassium, $Color)   % expect violet
? flame_color(calcium, $Color)     % expect red_orange
? flame_color($Metal, red)         % expect strontium ; lithium — one-to-many reverse
? flame_color($Metal, violet)      % expect potassium — reverse recall
? flame_color(copper, $Color)      % expect abstain — this source states no copper color
